      "latency_ms": 1646.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 744,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature change, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between molecules creates a “skin” on the surface, enabling phenomena like capillary action.<br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating bodies of water in winter. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 361 million km²). Found in oceans, rivers, lakes, glaciers, groundwater, and the atmosphere. |\n| **Biological role** | • Constitutes ~60 % of the human body (varies by age, sex, and fat content).<br>• Acts as a medium for biochemical reactions, transport of nutrients, waste removal, temperature regulation, and structural support in cells (cytoplasm). |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | • Drinking water and food preparation.<br>• Agriculture (irrigation).<br>• Power generation (hydropower, steam turbines).<br>• Manufacturing (cooling, solvents, cleaning).<br>• Transportation (ships, pipelines). |\n| **Environmental importance** | • Drives the **hydrologic (water) cycle**: evaporation → condensation → precipitation → runoff → infiltration → repeat.<br>• Regulates climate through heat transport and cloud formation.<br>• Supports ecosystems—from deep‑sea vents to tropical rainforests. |\n\n### Why water is unique\n1. **Polarity** gives it strong hydrogen‑bonding, which underlies many of its unusual physical properties (high boiling point, surface tension, etc.).\n2. **Hydrogen bonding** creates a dynamic network that can rearrange quickly, allowing water to flow easily while still maintaining cohesion.\n3. **Versatility as a solvent** enables it to dissolve salts, sugars, gases, and many organic molecules, making it the medium in which most biochemical reactions occur.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a tiny, polar molecule (H₂O) with remarkable physical and chemical properties that arise from hydrogen bonding. These properties make it an excellent solvent, a regulator of temperature, and a key player in Earth’s climate and life processes. Its abundance and versatility underpin virtually every natural and human‑made system on the planet."
